# Encoding comparison record

This is a synthetic measurement record for the supplied local module, not a claim about a production system or this machine. Assess the recorded conclusions without running a new benchmark or changing code.

Study A used timingBeforeConsumption for eager and lazy on the same 10,000 rows. Recorded median milliseconds were 20 and 0.2. A colleague claims a 100x end-to-end export speedup. Both outputs were later consumed and matched, but the timer placement is in runner.mjs.

Study B used timingAfterConsumption for eager and batched on the same 10,000 rows in alternating runs, same runtime, flags, warm-cache state and process conditions. The supplied counts/checksums cover all output and report zero errors. Encoding milliseconds were eager [20, 21, 19, 20, 20] and batched [10, 10, 11, 9, 10]. End-to-end request milliseconds were eager [100, 101, 99, 100, 100] and batched [90, 91, 89, 90, 90]. The supplied separate profile attributes the encoding work to JSON serialization and chunk concatenation on one CPU core. Treat this as supplied evidence, not a profile you personally captured.

The decision is whether the record supports each claimed speedup: Study A's 100x whole-export claim and Study B's approximately half the encoding time / 10% shorter end-to-end time for this workload. No request to select a library for a different workload is implied. State evidence limits; do not infer fleet reliability or claim you reran these measurements.
